<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-comparison" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Comparison Tests</title>

  <p>In proving that <m>p</m>-series converge in <xref ref="sec-series"/>, 
    we implicitly made use of the following.
  </p>

  <theorem>
    <title>Comparison Test</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Let <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty a_n</m> and 
        <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty b_n</m> be two series.
        <ul>
          <li>If <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty b_n</m> converges and 
            <m>0 \leq a_n \leq b_n</m> for all <m>n</m>, then <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty a_n</m> also converges.
          </li>
          <li>If <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty b_n</m> diverges and 
            <m>a_n \geq b_n \geq 0</m> for all <m>n</m>, then <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty a_n</m> also diverges.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        First, suppose  
          <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty b_n</m> converges to <m>S</m> and 
          <m>0 \leq a_n \leq b_n</m> for all <m>n</m>. Then for every <m>k</m>,
          <md>\sum _{n=1} ^k a_n \leq \sum _{n=1} ^k b_n \leq S</md>,
          so the series <m>\sum _{n=1} ^\infty a_n</m> is a bounded, monotonic
          sequence and hence converges.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now suppose instead <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty b_n</m> diverges and 
        <m>a_n \geq b_n \geq 0</m> for all <m>n</m>.  Suppose <m>L</m> is any real number.
        Then since <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty b_n</m> is increasing and divergent,
        there exists <m>k</m> such that <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^k b_n \gt L</m>.  
        Then <md>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^k a_n \geq \sum _{n=1} ^k b_n \gt L</md>.
        Since the series gets larger than any real number, we see that <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^k a_n</m> diverges as well.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>

  <remark>
    <p>Because the first finitely many terms don't affect whether a sequence or series
      converges or not, the comparison test can be strengthened: it is enough to know that
      <em>eventually</em> <m>a_n \leq b_n</m> (i.e. there exists <m>N</m> such that 
      <m>a_n \leq b_n</m> for all <m>n\gt N</m>).
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example>
    <p>Consider the series <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty \dfrac{1}{n-3}</m>.
    Since <m>n-3 \lt n</m>, <m>\frac{1}{n-3} \gt \frac{1}{n}</m>.  Then
    since the harmonic series diverges, the series <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty \dfrac{1}{n-3}</m>
    diverges as well.
    </p>
  </example>

  <example>
    <p>Consider the series <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty \dfrac{1}{n^2+5}</m>.
    Since <m>n^2+5 \gt n^2</m>, <m>\frac{1}{n^2+5} \lt \frac{1}{n^2}</m>.  Since this
    <m>p</m>-series converges, we have <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty \dfrac{1}{n^2+5}</m>
    must also converge.
    </p>
  </example>

  <activity>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Use the comparison test to determine if each of the following series converge or diverge.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=2} ^\infty \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{n-1}}</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty \dfrac{1}{n^3+n}</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty \dfrac{1}{e^n+e}</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty \dfrac{1}{n^2-n+1}</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </activity>

  <remark>
    <p>As we saw in the last part of the previous activity, direct comparison
    is not always effective, even if qualitatively one series seems to behave
    like another.</p>
  </remark>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-limit-comparison">
    <title>Limit Comparison Test</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty a_n</m> and
        <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty b_n</m> be two series 
        with <m>a_n \gt 0</m> and <m>b_n \gt 0</m> for all <m>n</m>.
        Suppose that <m>\displaystyle \lim _{n \rightarrow \infty} \dfrac{a_n}{b_n}=c</m>
        for some <m>c \gt 0</m>.  
        Then either <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty a_n</m> and
        <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty b_n</m> both converge, or 
        they both diverge.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>By the definition of the limit of a sequence, there exists <m>N</m> such that
      for <m>n \geq N</m>, <m>\dfrac{c}{2} \lt \dfrac{a_n}{b_n} \lt \dfrac{3c}{2}</m>.
      If <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty b_n</m> diverges, then we have <m>a_n \gt \left(\dfrac{c}{2}\right)b_n</m>,
      so <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty a_n</m> also diverges by the direct comparison test.  If instead <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty b_n</m> converges,
      then <m>a_n \lt \left(\dfrac{3c}{2}\right)b_n</m>, so <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty a_n</m> converges
      by direct comparison test.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>

  <example>
    <p>Since <m>\displaystyle \lim _{n \rightarrow \infty} \dfrac{n^2}{n^2-n+1}=1</m> and
    the <m>p</m>-series <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty \dfrac{1}{n^2}</m> converges,
    by the limit comparison test we know that <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty \dfrac{1}{n^2-n+1}</m>
    also converges.
    </p>
  </example>
  
  <activity>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Use an appropriate comparison test to determine if each of the following series converge or diverge.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=2} ^\infty \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{n+3}}</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty \dfrac{1}{n^4-n^2+7}</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty \dfrac{1}{2^n-n+5}</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty \dfrac{1}{n^3-n^2-1}</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </activity>

</section>